---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import SourcesStandards from '../../components/SourcesStandards.astro';
import { techArticleSchema } from '../../lib/techarticle.js';

const jsonLd = techArticleSchema({
  sourcePath: 'src/pages/safety/h2s.astro',
  path: '/safety/h2s',
  headline: "H2S Safety on Oil Rigs — Exposure Limits & Escape Procedures",
  description: "Hydrogen sulfide (H2S) safety guide for oil rig workers: exposure levels, OSHA and ACGIH limits, detection, personal monitors, SCBA use, and emergency escape procedures.",
  lang: 'en',
  extra: { about: "Hydrogen sulfide (H2S) workplace safety" },
});
---
<BaseLayout
  title="H2S Safety on Oil Rigs — Exposure Limits & Escape Procedures"
  description="Hydrogen sulfide (H2S) safety guide for oil rig workers: exposure levels, detection, personal monitors, SCBA use, and emergency escape procedures."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Safety', href: '/safety' }, { label: 'H2S Safety', href: '/safety/h2s' }]}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/safety/h2s"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/safety/h2s" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Safety — Sour Gas</div>
    <h1>H₂S <span>Safety</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">Hydrogen sulfide is one of the deadliest hazards in oil and gas — colorless, heavier than air, and capable of incapacitating a person in a single breath at high concentration.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="what-makes-hs-so-dangerous">What Makes H₂S So Dangerous</h2>
        <p>Hydrogen sulfide is a naturally occurring gas found in "sour" crude oil and natural gas reservoirs. It's colorless, flammable, and heavier than air — meaning it collects in low-lying areas, cellars, and confined spaces rather than dispersing. Its most dangerous property is olfactory fatigue: at low concentrations it smells like rotten eggs, but at higher, more dangerous concentrations it deadens your sense of smell almost instantly, removing the very warning sign that would otherwise alert you.</p>

        <h2 id="exposure-levels-effects">Exposure Levels & Effects</h2>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Concentration</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>0.5–5 ppm</td><td>Noticeable rotten egg odor</td></tr>
              <tr><td>10–20 ppm</td><td>Eye and respiratory irritation; 10 ppm is NIOSH's recommended exposure limit and a common personal-monitor alarm setpoint. OSHA's regulated limit is a 20 ppm ceiling (29 CFR 1910.1000, Table Z-2), not an "action level" — see the alarm response and the full Table Z-2 figures below.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>50–100 ppm</td><td>Loss of smell (olfactory fatigue) within minutes; serious eye damage risk</td></tr>
              <tr><td>100–150 ppm</td><td>Coughing, loss of smell entirely; immediately dangerous to life and health (IDLH)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>500–700 ppm</td><td>Loss of consciousness within minutes; respiratory failure risk</td></tr>
              <tr><td>700+ ppm</td><td>Rapid unconsciousness and death, potentially within a single breath</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="note-box">
          <strong id="worked-example">Worked example:</strong> A personal H₂S monitor on the rig floor reads 18 ppm. Checking the table above, that falls in the 10–20 ppm band shown above — eye and respiratory irritation. It's also already above the 10 ppm low-alarm setpoint most personal monitors use, so even though the effect at 18 ppm is mild, a monitor reading this is a real alarm, not a nuisance reading — stop the task and follow the alarm response below, don't wait for symptoms to confirm it. If the same monitor later spikes to 120 ppm near a flowback tank, that's inside the 100–150 ppm IDLH band from the table: smell has already disappeared entirely, and the correct response is immediate SCBA and evacuation to the muster point, not a second sniff test to confirm.
        </div>

        <div class="danger-box">
          <strong>Critical safety point:</strong> Never rely on smell alone to judge H₂S concentration. The gas disables your ability to smell it well before it reaches lethal levels. Personal and fixed gas detectors are the only reliable method of monitoring exposure.
        </div>

        <h2 id="alarm-response">What to Do When a Monitor Alarms at 10 ppm</h2>
        <p>10 ppm is the low-alarm setpoint on most personal H₂S monitors, matching NIOSH's recommended exposure limit — it is a warning to act, not a level to wait out or work through:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Stop the task and move immediately away from the source, crosswind or upwind if outdoors.</li>
          <li>Check the monitor's trend, not just the instantaneous reading — a number still climbing means the source is still releasing, even if the current reading looks tolerable.</li>
          <li>Notify your supervisor and any gas-testing personnel before continuing any work in the area.</li>
          <li>Do not re-enter or resume work until the area is tested clear per the site's H₂S safety plan, not on a personal judgment call that the smell seems to have faded.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="regulatory-limits">OSHA and ACGIH Limits, in Full</h2>
        <p>Two separate sets of numbers govern H₂S exposure, and they are not interchangeable:</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Limit</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>OSHA (29 CFR 1910.1000, Table Z-2)</td><td>20 ppm acceptable ceiling</td><td>Must not be exceeded at any time during an 8-hour shift. This is the enforceable federal limit.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>OSHA (same table)</td><td>50 ppm acceptable maximum peak, 10 minutes</td><td>A single excursion above the 20 ppm ceiling, up to 50 ppm, is allowed for a maximum of 10 minutes once per 8-hour shift — but only if no other measurable H₂S exposure occurs during that shift. It is a narrow, one-time exception, not a routine working range.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>ACGIH TLV</td><td>1 ppm TWA / 5 ppm STEL</td><td>The American Conference of Governmental Industrial Hygienists' recommended guideline — far more conservative than OSHA's limit, but not itself a legal requirement in the US unless adopted by a specific jurisdiction or company policy. 1 ppm is the 8-hour time-weighted average; 5 ppm is the short-term (15-minute) exposure limit.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--fog);">OSHA's PEL and ACGIH's TLV are two different standards from two different bodies — a workplace that is compliant with OSHA's 20 ppm ceiling is not automatically within ACGIH's far lower 1 ppm TWA recommendation. Company H₂S programs commonly adopt the stricter ACGIH figures as an internal policy even where OSHA's higher limit is the only legal floor.</p>

        <h2 id="detection-monitoring">Detection & Monitoring</h2>
        <p>Rigs operating in known or suspected sour gas fields deploy layered detection: fixed gas detection systems throughout the rig (particularly near the wellhead, shakers, and any low-lying or enclosed areas), plus personal H₂S monitors worn by every individual on location. Personal monitors alarm audibly and visually well before concentrations become dangerous, giving workers time to evacuate to a briefing area or don SCBA.</p>

        <h2 id="emergency-response-equipment">Emergency Response Equipment</h2>
        <p><strong>SCBA (Self-Contained Breathing Apparatus)</strong> provides a fully independent air supply and is the standard emergency response equipment for H₂S incidents — staged at strategic locations around the rig for rapid access. Cascade air systems and escape packs (smaller, shorter-duration SCBA units) are also common, giving workers enough air to reach a safe muster point rather than a full-duration supply.</p>

        <h2 id="training-requirements">Training Requirements</h2>
        <p>Anyone working on a well designated as having H₂S potential must complete H₂S safety training before being on location — typically a one-day course covering the physiological effects, detector use, SCBA donning under time pressure, and site-specific emergency response procedures. This is separate from general offshore safety training like <a href="/careers/certifications">BOSIET</a>, and is specifically required whenever sour gas is a credible risk, onshore or offshore.</p>

        <h2 id="if-you-suspect-an-hs-release">If You Suspect an H₂S Release</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Move immediately crosswind, then upwind, and to higher ground if outdoors — H₂S sinks and pools in low areas.</li>
          <li>Do not attempt to rescue a downed coworker without SCBA — multiple-casualty H₂S incidents commonly happen when a would-be rescuer is overcome attempting an unprotected rescue.</li>
          <li>Sound the alarm and proceed to the designated muster point immediately.</li>
          <li>Report to a supervisor and follow the site-specific H₂S emergency response plan.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A near-miss or an unsafe H₂S response is exactly the kind of thing worth logging on a <a href="/blog/stop-card-system">STOP card</a> — it's how a rig turns one close call into a fix for the whole crew.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
